  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1114 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n00Z upper air analysis and GOES Water Vapor satellite trends\nshowed a deep upper-level trough in place across the eastern\nUnited States late this evening with an upper-level ridge over\nthe Rockies. Late evening surface analysis placed a 1028 mb\nsurface high centered near Hudson Bay. This surface high was\nbringing strong low-level cold air advection to the Mid-South\nwith late evening winds remaining elevated thus far.\n\nA deep upper trough and strong surface high will bring the\ncoldest air of the season thus far to the Mid-South. Friday\nmorning temperatures will drop into the upper 20s to middle 30s,\nwith the lowest temperatures expected across portions of West\nTennessee and north Mississippi. The coldest temperatures are\nexpected to occur Friday night with lows expected to be at or\nbelow freezing areawide. NBM probabilities show a medium to high\nchance (50-70%) of temperatures at or below 28 degrees mostly\nacross West Tennessee by Saturday morning. These cold morning\ntemperatures are expected to persist into Sunday. Highs each day \nwill recover into the 50s.\n\nThankfully, the latest long-term deterministic and ensemble model\nsolutions show this upper-level trough lifting out of the eastern\nU.S. as an upper-level ridge builds from the Rockies into the\nCentral U.S. next week. This will result in continued dry weather\nwith a gradual moderation of temperatures and a potential for\nhighs approaching 70 degrees in a few locations towards the end\nof next week.",
